<project xmlns="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0" xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance" xsi:schemaLocation="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0 http://maven.apache.org/xsd/maven-4.0.0.xsd">
    <modelVersion>4.0.0</modelVersion>
    <parent>
        <groupId>org.openidentityplatform.openam</groupId>
        <artifactId>openam-ui</artifactId>
        <version>16.2.0-SNAPSHOT</version>
    </parent>

    <artifactId>openam-ui-ria</artifactId>
    <name>OpenAM RIA Web UI</name>
    <packaging>pom</packaging>

    <properties>
        <project.build.sourceEncoding>UTF-8</project.build.sourceEncoding>
        <!--
            Applies to the tgz:npm artifacts copied by copy-commons-npm-tarballs below, which
            are now the only thing it needs to apply to.

            TASK 4.7 CLOSED THE SPLIT THIS COMMENT USED TO DESCRIBE. The commons.ui:user:zip:www
            dependency is gone, so the build no longer composes AMD/www sources at
            3.2.0-SNAPSHOT over vendor libraries pinned at 3.1.2 by a BOM this property could
            not reach. There is only one commons version in the build now. The vendor libraries
            come from npm, at versions pinned in package.json and package-lock.json, and they
            are no longer coupled to any commons release at all - which is also why a vendor
            bump in commons 3.2 no longer needs to reach AM.

            PREREQUISITE: no released commons carries a tgz:npm artifact, so these resolve from
            ~/.m2 only. Build them first, from the sibling commons checkout on branch
            features/ui-migration:
                mvn install -f ui/pom.xml
            Without that this module fails at `initialize` with "Could not find artifact
            org.openidentityplatform.commons.ui:commons:tgz:npm:3.2.0-SNAPSHOT".

            TASK 4.8 ENDED THE commons.ui.libs HAZARD ALTOGETHER. The warning that stood here
            through 4.7 - build from ../ only, and never `mvn clean` on openam-ui or `mvn ... -am`
            from the OpenAM root, because clean-external DELETES artifacts from ~/.m2 that are
            published nowhere - applied to the one artifact 4.7 left standing, the CodeMirror zip.
            That artifact is gone, and with it maven-external-dependency-plugin, its
            clean-external goal, and the reason this module could not be built on its own. Both
            `mvn clean` and a standalone build here are safe again. The prerequisite above is not:
            the two tgz:npm commons artifacts still resolve from ~/.m2 only.
        -->
        <commons.ui.version>3.2.0-SNAPSHOT</commons.ui.version>
    </properties>

    <!--
        THIS MODULE HAS NO MAVEN DEPENDENCIES. Task 4.7 retired the commons.ui.libs channel - 24
        dependencies (20 runtime, 4 test) plus the commons.ui:user:zip:www dependency that carried
        33 more of them transitively - and task 4.8 took the one it left standing, the CodeMirror
        zip. Every runtime library is now a declared npm dependency in package.json, pinned in
        package-lock.json, staged from node_modules into target/npm-libs by vite.config.js and
        visible to `npm audit`, as ui-build-and-packaging's "Runtime libraries are managed package
        dependencies" requires. The per-file table is in NOTES-libs-retire.md; CodeMirror's four
        JS and two CSS files are mapped in NPM_LIBRARY_FILES beside the rest.

        The commons sources are not dependencies either: they arrive as the two tgz:npm artifacts
        copy-commons-npm-tarballs copies and npm-install-commons installs.
    -->

    <build>
        <resources>
            <resource>
                <directory>${basedir}/src/main/js</directory>
            </resource>
            <resource>
                <directory>${basedir}/src/main/resources</directory>
                <filtering>true</filtering>
            </resource>
        </resources>

        <testResources>
            <testResource>
                <directory>${basedir}/src/test/js</directory>
            </testResource>
            <testResource>
                <directory>${basedir}/src/test/resources</directory>
            </testResource>
        </testResources>

        <plugins>
            <plugin>
                <artifactId>maven-resources-plugin</artifactId>
                <version>2.7</version>
                <dependencies>
                    <dependency>
                        <groupId>org.apache.maven.shared</groupId>
                        <artifactId>maven-filtering</artifactId>
                        <version>1.3</version>
                    </dependency>
                </dependencies>
                <executions>
                    <execution>
                        <phase>process-resources</phase>
                        <goals>
                            <goal>resources</goal>
                            <goal>testResources</goal>
                        </goals>
                    </execution>
                </executions>
            </plugin>

            <plugin>
                <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
                <artifactId>maven-dependency-plugin</artifactId>
                <executions>
                    <execution>
                        <id>copy-commons-npm-tarballs</id>
                        <phase>initialize</phase>
                        <goals>
                            <goal>copy</goal>
                        </goals>
                        <configuration>
                            <outputDirectory>${project.build.directory}/npm</outputDirectory>
                            <!--
                                destFileName strips the version so the npm-install-commons
                                arguments below can hardcode the path. That makes the copy
                                destination stable across versions, so the overWrite flags have
                                to be explicit: with dependency:copy's defaults
                                (overWriteSnapshots=false, overWriteIfNewer=true) a re-pinned
                                commons.ui.version whose artifact has an older mtime would
                                silently leave the previous tarball in place.
                            -->
                            <overWriteReleases>true</overWriteReleases>
                            <overWriteSnapshots>true</overWriteSnapshots>
                            <artifactItems>
                                <artifactItem>
                                    <groupId>org.openidentityplatform.commons.ui</groupId>
                                    <artifactId>commons</artifactId>
                                    <version>${commons.ui.version}</version>
                                    <type>tgz</type>
                                    <classifier>npm</classifier>
                                    <destFileName>ui-commons.tgz</destFileName>
                                </artifactItem>
                                <artifactItem>
                                    <groupId>org.openidentityplatform.commons.ui</groupId>
                                    <artifactId>user</artifactId>
                                    <version>${commons.ui.version}</version>
                                    <type>tgz</type>
                                    <classifier>npm</classifier>
                                    <destFileName>ui-user.tgz</destFileName>
                                </artifactItem>
                            </artifactItems>
                        </configuration>
                    </execution>
                    <!--
                        TASK 4.8 REMOVED unpack-codemirror, THE LAST UNPACK EXECUTION HERE. It
                        unpacked commons.ui.libs:CodeMirror:zip:4.10 into ${project.build.directory}
                        for dir.xml's six fileSets to cherry-pick; CodeMirror is now an npm
                        dependency staged by vite.config.js like every other runtime library.
                        This plugin no longer unpacks anything into target/ at all; the copy
                        above is its only execution left. target/dependencies was never this
                        plugin's output - it was maven-assembly-plugin's, from prepare-working-dir,
                        which 4.8 removed as well.
                    -->

                </executions>
            </plugin>
            <plugin>
                <groupId>com.github.eirslett</groupId>
                <artifactId>frontend-maven-plugin</artifactId>
                <executions>
                    <!--
                        Installs the two commons packages out of band. They are deliberately NOT
                        in package.json: npm pins file: tarballs by content hash, and a
                        3.2.0-SNAPSHOT tarball is one version string over changing bytes, so the
                        integrity would go stale on every commons rebuild - `npm ci` hard-fails
                        and `npm install` rewrites the lockfile.

                        ORDER IS LOAD-BEARING AND IS NOT WHAT THIS FILE SHOWS. This execution is
                        declared first but must RUN LAST, after install-node-and-npm and
                        npm-install. It does, because Maven's pluginManagement merge injects the
                        executions inherited from openam-ui/pom.xml ahead of module-declared
                        ones. Move npm-install into this pom, or reorder it, and the order flips:
                        a plain `npm install` reconciles node_modules against package.json and
                        prunes both packages ("removed 50 packages"). Grunt's copy:compose then
                        drops the missing source directories SILENTLY and the build fails much
                        later in requirejs:compile for an unrelated-looking reason. The guard in
                        Gruntfile.js turns that into a named failure; do not rely on it instead
                        of preserving the order.

                        The legacy-peer-deps flag is required, not cosmetic. Both packages declare
                        their runtime libraries as peerDependencies, and npm 7+ auto-installs
                        those: without the flag the build resolves 50 unpinned packages (react,
                        backbone, handlebars, jquery, ...) from the public registry on every run,
                        recorded in neither package.json nor package-lock.json because the
                        no-save flag suppresses exactly that record. Since task 4.7 AM declares
                        these libraries itself, at exact versions, in package.json - so an
                        auto-installed peer would now be a SECOND, unpinned copy resolved over
                        the top of the audited one, which is worse than the duplicate this
                        comment used to describe, not better. With the flag, npm adds 2 packages
                        and stays offline. Do NOT substitute the omit=peer flag: ui-user declares
                        ui-commons itself as an optional peer, so omitting peers silently drops
                        ui-commons.
                    -->
                    <execution>
                        <id>npm-install-commons</id>
                        <goals>
                            <goal>npm</goal>
                        </goals>
                        <phase>initialize</phase>
                        <configuration>
                            <arguments>install ${project.build.directory}/npm/ui-commons.tgz ${project.build.directory}/npm/ui-user.tgz --no-save --legacy-peer-deps</arguments>
                        </configuration>
                    </execution>
                    <!--
                        Runs the Vite build. The script name is unchanged from the Grunt pipeline
                        on purpose: build:production is what this execution has always called, so
                        flipping the pipeline is a package.json edit, not a pom edit.

                        The version no longer travels as a CLI argument. Grunt read it with
                        grunt.option("target-version"); Vite has no equivalent CLI option parsing,
                        so vite.config.js reads process.env.TARGET_VERSION instead, defaulting to
                        "dev" exactly as Gruntfile.js did.

                        This execution is bound to compile, NOT initialize. That matters: the
                        ordering constraint described above governs the initialize-phase set only
                        (install-node-and-npm, npm-install, npm-install-commons). Adding any new
                        npm execution at initialize would join that set and must then be declared
                        so it still runs after npm-install-commons - otherwise the commons
                        packages are pruned. Unlike Grunt, Vite has no check-composition-sources
                        guard, so that failure would be silent.
                    -->
                    <execution>
                        <id>npm-build</id>
                        <goals>
                            <goal>npm</goal>
                        </goals>
                        <phase>compile</phase>
                        <configuration>
                            <arguments>run build:production</arguments>
                            <environmentVariables>
                                <TARGET_VERSION>${project.version}</TARGET_VERSION>
                            </environmentVariables>
                        </configuration>
                    </execution>
                    <!--
                        Runs the unit suite. `npm run test` is `npm run test:unit` is `vitest run`
                        over src/test/vitest/**; task 9.1 repointed it. The execution itself is NOT
                        this migration's: it arrived upstream in 9a9a1ac44b ("Run frontend Karma
                        tests during the Maven test phase", #902, 2025-08-07), when `npm test` still
                        meant `grunt karma:build`, and it is present on master byte for byte as it
                        stands here. Flipping Karma to Vitest was a package.json edit, not a pom
                        edit, for the same reason build:production was above.

                        -DskipTests SKIPS THIS EXECUTION, and that is load-bearing rather than
                        incidental. frontend-maven-plugin 1.15.4 binds the npm mojo's skipTests
                        parameter to the ${skipTests} user property, and AbstractFrontendMojo skips
                        when skipTests is true AND the execution's DECLARED phase is test or
                        integration-test. This one declares test, so a build passing the flag logs
                        the npm-test execution header immediately followed by `[INFO] Skipping
                        execution.` and carries on. Two consequences are easy to get wrong:

                          * -Dmaven.test.skip is NOT a synonym. Only ${skipTests} is bound to this
                            mojo, so a build using maven.test.skip still runs the whole suite.
                          * npm-build is bound to compile, not test, so it runs either way. The Vite
                            build, and therefore the zip that zip.xml packs, are unaffected by the flag.
                            That is precisely what the documented XUI build paths rely on:
                            e2e/local/README.md (:78, :160, :177), .github/workflows/xui-e2e.yml
                            (:108), .github/workflows/xui-capture-drift.yml (:143) and
                            .github/workflows/xui-local-server.yml (:230) all build this module with
                            -DskipTests to produce or deploy the zip, and none of them runs this
                            suite. Do not "fix" that by removing the flag.

                        WHICH LANE RUNS IT ON EVERY PUSH AND PULL REQUEST:
                        .github/workflows/build.yml. That workflow runs `mvn verify` over the whole
                        reactor with no -DskipTests, so this suite executes on all nine of its matrix
                        cells. Task 9.3 chose that placement deliberately, over an ubuntu-only gate
                        and over a job of its own; the timings and the tradeoffs of all three are in
                        NOTES-test-wiring.md.

                        Two other workflows also reach the test phase without the flag, so a failure
                        here can surface in them too. Neither is the gate, and do not read build.yml
                        as the only place this suite runs:

                          * deploy.yml (`package` at :53, `deploy` at :60) is shielded in practice.
                            Its deploy-maven job is conditional on a successful Build workflow_run,
                            so while this suite is red that job never starts.
                          * release.yml (:57) is NOT shielded. It is workflow_dispatch and gated on
                            nothing, and maven-release-plugin 3.0.0-M7 is declared in the root pom
                            with its goals element commented out (pom.xml:1817), leaving
                            preparationGoals at the default `clean verify`. A release cut while this
                            suite is red therefore fails inside release:prepare.

                        THE SUITE IS RED ON PURPOSE TODAY, so `mvn verify` fails in this module:
                        ten of 163 cases, documented in the two test file headers and in commit
                        ade9c24878. Six execute a real lodash-4 defect, the `_.object` call in the
                        commons URIUtils.js, which `npm run verify:lib-split` already reports as an
                        error; four assert a require.toUrl branch that cannot be reached from a
                        Vitest module scope but is still live in production on the openam-oauth2
                        .ftl entry points. Neither set is task 9.3's to fix and neither is hidden.
                        Because this module builds well ahead of openam-server and
                        openam-distribution, that failure also stops build.yml's Upload artifacts
                        step and its build-docker job, which is where build.yml's own deployed-AM
                        Playwright coverage lives. If a reactor build has to get past it,
                        -Dmaven.test.failure.ignore=true IS honoured by this mojo in the test phase;
                        prefer fixing the six.
                    -->
                    <execution>
                        <id>npm-test</id>
                        <goals>
                            <goal>npm</goal>
                        </goals>
                        <phase>test</phase>
                        <configuration>
                            <arguments>run test</arguments>
                        </configuration>
                    </execution>
                </executions>
            </plugin>
            <plugin>
                <artifactId>maven-assembly-plugin</artifactId>
                <executions>
                    <!--
                        TASK 4.8 REMOVED prepare-working-dir AND DELETED src/main/assembly/dir.xml
                        WITH IT. That execution existed to run dir.xml as an assembly whose output
                        directory, target/dependencies, was composition source #1 for the frontend
                        build - so the descriptor was never packaging-only, it was a build input.
                        4.7 emptied it down to CodeMirror and 4.8 moved CodeMirror to npm, leaving
                        a descriptor with no fileSets. Deleting it is not tidying: an assembly that
                        produces an empty composition source is the silent-drop shape this build
                        refuses, and vite.config.js no longer lists the directory at all.

                        zip.xml is untouched and unrelated. It is the packaging descriptor D8
                        preserves, it packs target/compiled, and openam-server-only consumes what
                        it produces.
                    -->
                    <execution>
                        <id>build-final-zip</id>
                        <phase>package</phase>
                        <goals>
                            <goal>single</goal>
                        </goals>
                        <configuration>
                            <descriptors>
                                <descriptor>src/main/assembly/zip.xml</descriptor>
                            </descriptors>
                        </configuration>
                    </execution>
                </executions>
            </plugin>
        </plugins>
    </build>
</project>
